[- Performance: numbers, methodology, honest scope. -]
[page id='performance'
  [meta
    title='Performance'
    layout='reference'
    nav-order='720'
    audience='evaluator,user'
    summary='Numbers and the bench setup that produced them.'
  ]

  [section id='parse' title='Parse'
    [prose Parse throughput on a single core at v0.7.0-dev — CX at roughly 380 MB/s, XML at 290 MB/s, JSON at 510 MB/s, YAML at 95 MB/s. Bench setup: M2 Pro, libcx built with -prod, 1 MB fixture per format.]
  ]

  [section id='emit' title='Emit'
    [prose Emit throughput follows parse — same order of magnitude per format. The canonical-form emitter pays an extra normalisation pass and runs around 60 percent of the regular emit.]
  ]

  [section id='eval' title='Eval'
    [prose The CXL streaming evaluator runs at 340 MB/s on the comparable bench corpus — about 17 percent under the comparable JSON benchmark on the same workload. Bench setup mirrors the parse row above: M2 Pro, libcx built with -prod, 1 MB fixture. The v0.7.0 target was 300 MB/s; that line has been crossed.]
  ]

  [section id='binding-overhead' title='Binding Overhead'
    [prose FFI overhead per call is in the low microseconds. Python ctypes adds about 35 microseconds per parse-and-emit round-trip on a 1 KB input; Go cgo is similar; Rust bindgen is closer to one microsecond. See the per-binding API pages for the exact wrappers.]
  ]

  [section id='methodology' title='Methodology'
    [prose The bench is at vcx/tests/runners/streaming_bench.v and vcx/tests/runners/eval_features_bench.v. Run with make bench; results land in target/bench.json. The cross-binding parity check at scripts/run_bench_json.py compares per-binding times against the V baseline.]
  ]

